```css
/* 1. Global kill-switch: neutralize decorative motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}

/* 2. Better: enhance deliberately */
.fade-in {
  opacity: 1;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .fade-in {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: rise 0.6s ease-out forwards;
  }
  @keyframes rise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
}

/* 3. Pair with JS + CSS var for parallax */
:root { --parallax: 1; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root { --parallax: 0; }
}
/* JS: layer.style.transform = `translateY(${y * getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--parallax')}px)` */
```

**Why this works:** The reduce media query matches users who asked their OS for less motion (vestibular disorders, motion sickness) — for them, flashing slideshows and parallax are not an aesthetic issue but a health issue. The blanket `0.01ms` pattern is the safe default because it ends animations *immediately* while still firing `animationend` events (fully zeroing duration can break JS that waits for those events) and stops infinite loops with `iteration-count: 1`. The progressive-enhancement variant (option 2) is architecturally cleaner: motion exists only inside `no-preference`, so reduced-motion users get the finished state by default with no `!important` arms race. For JS-driven effects, mirror the preference with `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` or a CSS custom property the script reads, so CSS and JS never disagree. Note this replaces *decorative* motion only — functional feedback (e.g. a progress bar) should still exist, just without flourish.

